            <ArticleTitle>Optimizing local anesthesia for grommet insertion: eutectic mixture of local anaesthetics versus Ametop: a randomized clinical trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>658-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MAO.0b013e3181776749</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the effect of eutetic mixture of local anesthetics (EMLA) with Ametop in providing local anesthesia for grommet insertion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Double-blind randomized clinical trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-one adult patients diagnosed with otitis media with effusion were recruited into the trial and underwent grommet insertion under local anesthesia; 21 were randomly allocated to the EMLA group and 20 to the Ametop group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The primary outcome measure of the study was the level of pain experienced by the patient. The secondary outcome measure was the overall satisfaction with the procedure. Both measures were assessed using visual analogue scales.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Both the topical anesthetic agents provided good pain relief during grommet insertion. There was no statistically significant difference in the pain or satisfaction scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Both EMLA and Ametop can provide good local anesthetic effect during grommet insertion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
